FILE: app/src/pulse.ts
```ts
import {
  createPropertyAnimationClip,
  createPropertyAnimationGroup,
  playAnimation,
  stopAnimation,
} from "@babylonjs/lite";
import type { AnimationManager } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function createPulse(
  manager: AnimationManager,
  button: { scale: number },
): () => void {
  const clip = createPropertyAnimationClip("pulse", [
    {
      path: "scale",
      keys: [
        { time: 0, value: 1 },
        { time: 0.2, value: 1.5 },
        { time: 0.4, value: 1 },
      ],
    },
  ]);

  const group = createPropertyAnimationGroup(manager, button, clip, {
    loop: false,
    fromTime: 0,
    // use the clip's own (float32-rounded) duration so the final write is exactly 1
    toTime: clip.duration,
  });

  // The group starts playing on creation; hold it still until the first press.
  stopAnimation(group);

  return () => {
    // Restart from the beginning, also when a pulse is running or has finished.
    stopAnimation(group);
    playAnimation(group);
  };
}
```

I wrote `src/pulse.ts`. Each `createPulse` builds one non-looping property group on the button's `scale` (keys 1 → 1.5 → 1 at 0, 0.2 and 0.4 s) and stops it right away, because creation starts it playing. Each press calls `stopAnimation` then `playAnimation`, which restarts the pulse from the beginning, including after an earlier one has finished.

I couldn't run anything, so this is untested. I'm also assuming the group stays attached to the manager after it finishes, which the notes imply but don't state. On a press, `scale` isn't reset to 1 immediately. The pulse restarts at the manager's next update, which happens at most one frame later.
